-- title:
--   Path properties transfer from a configuration to any larger one
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d \in \mathbb{N}$. A *configuration* on $\mathbb{R}^d$ assigns to every point $x$ a double cone $\Gamma(x)$, i.e. a unit axis $v$ together with an apex angle $\vartheta_V \in (0, \pi/2]$; its *carrier* is the set
--   $$|\Gamma(x)| \;=\; \tilde V(v,\vartheta_V) \,\cup\, \bigl(-\tilde V(v,\vartheta_V)\bigr) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d, \qquad \tilde V(v,\vartheta) \;=\; \Bigl\{h \ne 0 \;:\; \cos\vartheta < \tfrac{\langle v, h\rangle}{\lVert h\rVert}\Bigr\},$$
--   and $V^{\Gamma}[x] = x + |\Gamma(x)|$. The graph attached to $\Gamma$ on the lattice has vertex set $\mathbb{Z}^d$, two distinct lattice points $x, y$ being adjacent when $y \in V^{\Gamma}[x]$ or $x \in V^{\Gamma}[y]$.
--
--   For $N, M \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda, R_0 \in \mathbb{R}$, $\mathrm{PathPropsLong}(\Gamma, N, M, \lambda, R_0)$ is the strengthened package of claims of Theorem 5.15: there is a family of walks $p_{xy}$ in that graph, one running from $x$ to $y$ for *every* ordered pair $(x,y)$ of lattice points, such that
--
--   * $\operatorname{length}(p_{xy}) \le N$;
--   * for every unoriented edge $e$, the set of pairs $\{(x,y) : e \in \operatorname{edges}(p_{xy})\}$ has cardinality at most $M$ (as an element of $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$);
--   * every dart $(a,b)$ of every $p_{xy}$ satisfies
--   $$\lambda^{-1}\lVert x - y\rVert \;\le\; \lVert a - b\rVert \;\le\; \lambda\,\lVert x - y\rVert \qquad\text{and}\qquad R_0 < \lVert a - b\rVert .$$
--
--   The last inequality is the "long" strengthening.
--
--   Let now $\Gamma$ and $\Gamma'$ be two configurations on $\mathbb{R}^d$ (with $\mathbb{R}^d$ carrying the Euclidean inner product), and suppose that $\Gamma'$ is pointwise smaller than $\Gamma$:
--   $$|\Gamma'(x)| \subseteq |\Gamma(x)| \qquad \text{for every } x \in \mathbb{R}^d .$$
--   Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{PathPropsLong}(\Gamma', N, M, \lambda, R_0) \implies \mathrm{PathPropsLong}(\Gamma, N, M, \lambda, R_0),$$
--
--   with the *same* parameters $N$, $M$, $\lambda$, $R_0$.
--
--   The result is the transfer step that lets the hard construction be carried out for a conveniently normalized configuration — one realizing only boundedly many cone types — and then exported to an arbitrary configuration whose cones contain those of the normalized one. The mechanism is that shrinking the cones shrinks the associated graph, so every walk of the smaller graph is still a walk of the larger one, with the same endpoints, length and edge lengths.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis compares only the *carriers* of the two double cones, i.e. the underlying sets, and says nothing about their axes or apex angles; this is the coarser (and correct) notion, since the graph depends only on the sets. The parameters are left completely unconstrained: no positivity is assumed of $\lambda$ or $R_0$ here, since any such constraints live inside the predicate itself.
